WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.000
Brainstorm. A live map of your code,
and the agents writing it.

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:14.000
One agent, you can follow. Three, and the changes
scatter across your project.

00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:20.000
A wall of edits tells you what changed.
Understanding why takes more.

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:26.000
In one coding study, AI users
scored lower on comprehension.

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:31.000
Those who sought explanations understood more.
That's the habit we're designing for.

00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:38.000
Follow a session. Open an edit. Ask why.

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:45.000
Then zoom out to see where agents are working
and how your files connect.

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:50.000
Underneath, local listeners read session logs.

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:54.000
A mapper follows files and imports.
SQLite keeps the record.

00:00:54.000 --> 00:01:00.000
Nemotron on our Brev GPU
summarizes code and labels steps.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:05.000
Claude answers questions from a small package
of relevant context.

00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:12.000
On our Hono benchmark: three hundred eleven files,
seventy-three seconds, about two cents of GPU time.

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:17.000
Startup and idle time are extra.

00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:23.000
Re-read only changed files. Keep each question focused.
The map itself uses no model tokens.

00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:30.000
Let the agents type.
Keep your brain in the loop.
